{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "2",
  "band": "K-2",
  "focus": "Grade 2 owns flexible meaning. Children meet words with more than one sense (bark, spring, watch), words that say exactly how someone speaks or thinks (mumble, announce, predict), richer feelings and character traits (disappointed, jealous, stubborn, loyal), the language of comparing (similar, contrast, -er and -est), and the first nonfiction vocabulary in math, science, social studies, and reading. It extends the two derived-word patterns from grade 1 with re- and comparing endings. It differs from grade 1, which concentrates on describing, sequence, and cause words, and from grade 3, which adds more abstract academic verbs, longer multi-syllable words, and the first Latin and Greek word parts.",
  "selection": "Words were chosen for how often second graders need them to say what they think and to read simple nonfiction, how many other words they unlock, and how well they move across languages. Multiple-meaning words appear in two units so children practice choosing the right sense from context. Connectors (while, until, although, however) and comparison words (than, both, neither) are grouped as function words because they are the hardest small words for multilingual learners. Four disciplinary units cover reading nonfiction, math story problems, matter in science, and maps in social studies; Tier 3 words (fable, folktale, diagram, caption, difference, estimate, solid, liquid, map, globe) are limited to two per unit. Morphology stays within re- and -er/-est, with plural and -ed/-ing practice woven through the verb units. Deliberately left for grade 1: weather and habitat words and simple sequence and cause words. Deliberately left for grade 3: abstract verbs, figurative language, and Latin and Greek roots.",
  "arc": [
    "Talking Verbs",
    "Big Feelings",
    "Words with Two Meanings",
    "Words That Join Ideas",
    "Fables and Folktales",
    "Compare and Contrast",
    "Comparing with -er and -est",
    "Thinking Verbs",
    "How Nonfiction Works",
    "Math Story Words",
    "Story Characters",
    "More Words with Two Meanings",
    "Solids, Liquids, and Change",
    "Do It Again with re-",
    "Maps and Places"
  ],
